Work in ZF set theory, without assuming the axiom of choice. The partition principle says that whenever there is a surjection $f:A\to B$, there is an injection $g:B\to A$. It does not require $f(g(b))=b$. The axiom of choice asserts a choice function for every family of nonempty sets. Ask whether ZF proves that the former principle implies the latter. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.03664}{[S2]}

